[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc1
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is pc1’s host routing configuration. pc1’s interface eth0 is correct (195.11.14.2/24), and it can ARP and ping its on-link gateway 195.11.14.1, but its default route points to a non-existent next-hop 195.11.14.254. Evidence: pc1’s routing table shows “default via 195.11.14.254 dev eth0,” and ARP resolution for that next-hop fails (“195.11.14.254 dev eth0 FAILED”). An ip route lookup for 200.1.1.2 confirms traffic is sent “via 195.11.14.254 dev eth0,” so packets to any off-subnet address cannot be forwarded. Linux returns “Destination Host Unreachable” because it cannot resolve the configured next-hop’s MAC.

Mechanism and symptoms: pc1 can ping 195.11.14.1 (on-link), but any off-subnet destination (e.g., 200.1.1.2) is attempted via 195.11.14.254, which fails at ARP, generating local unreachable errors. From the partner side, pings from pc2 to pc1 time out: the echo request reaches pc1 via the routers (BGP is correct on router1/router2), but pc1’s echo reply to 200.1.1.2 again follows the bad default and never leaves pc1.

Localization: pc1 only; routers have correct routes (router1 sees BGP 200.1.1.0/24 via router2, and router2 sees 195.11.14.0/24 via router1).

Root cause: pc1’s default gateway was misconfigured to 195.11.14.254 during “cleanup.”

Corrective fix: on pc1, replace the default route with the real gateway:
- ip route replace default via 195.11.14.1 dev eth0
Also update persistent network config to use 195.11.14.1 going forward.
=== END ===